  "churn_control_note": "dominance and stability survive the fixed-span control; core_n does not and was retired as a series in issue #5. This issue the controlled dominance step is +0.5 pt at the 7-day span (91.3 -> 91.8) against #5's +5.5, and +0.8 at the 5-day span. Per ELAPSED DAY the widths disagree on whether the climb is slowing (7-day 2.8 -> 1.8 -> 0.5/day; 5-day 2.1 -> 0.4 -> 0.8/day), so no rate claim is made. Controlled stability ticks UP for the first time (1.24 -> 1.25 at 7 days, flat at 1.32 over 5) - the first non-decline in that series.",
  "permeability_note": "RESOLVED as an instrument question. A cohort's fixed-horizon cell is frozen once its N-day window closes, so the issue-over-issue running mean can move ONLY by cohort entry. The per-cohort identity check across the #5->#6 boundary HOLDS at all three horizons, and recomputing #6 over each horizon's SHARED cohorts reproduces #5's cells exactly (32.2 / 35.7 / 36.8). The shared sets differ by horizon - 10 at N=3 (none entered), 9 at N=4 (08-15 entered), 8 at N=5 (08-14 entered) - so only N=3 is a like-for-like cohort set; at N=4 and N=5 the gap to the all-cohort cell IS the entering cohort. Separately, the published day-window average drew on an IDENTICAL ten cohorts across #5->#6 and still rose 42.9 -> 43.9, which with membership constant can only be observation length: the original confound demonstrated live. The running mean is therefore retired as a behavioural series; the object that carries behaviour is the per-cohort conversion sequence, now tested directly."
